[h1]Better Commerce Screen UI by Najane[/h1]

The Commerce screen decides what every settlement in your empire lives on - and the game makes you do it one drag at a time, with the numbers you need spread over two columns and a scrollbar.

This mod makes it workable. [b]No rules change[/b]: everything it does you could do by hand - it just takes far longer.

[hr][/hr]
[h2]What you get[/h2]

[list]
[*][b]The mouse does more[/b] - right-click an assigned resource to return it to the pool, and hold [b]Shift[/b] to do it to every resource of that kind. Shift while assigning fills a settlement instead of placing one. A [b]?[/b] by the buttons lists the shortcuts, since none can be found by looking.
[*][b]Assign All, Reassign All, Unassign All[/b], plus a priority picker and quick assign on each settlement card.
[*][b]Optional automation[/b] for resources arriving with the screen closed. Off until you change it.
[*][b]Trade routes at a glance[/b] - origin, destination and domain on one title line, three to a row, plus a total of the routes you could still open.
[*][b]Empire resources in numbers[/b] - not "+1 Gold in all settlements" but what that is worth to you.
[*][b]A new Factory Resources tab[/b] for the Modern age: what slotted resources produce, what idle ones would add.
[*][b]A running GDP total[/b] - roughly what your assigned Resources earn per turn towards the Economic Victory, broken down by source.
[*][b]A calmer screen[/b] - icon tabs, no instruction paragraph, and no "resources can be assigned" nag while nothing you hold can go anywhere.
[/list]

[hr][/hr]
[h2]How Assign All decides[/h2]

[b]Unhappiness first[/b] (switchable): any settlement below zero Happiness is fed back up to zero, cities before towns, and the help is spread rather than dumped - settlements at -10, -20 and -30 are levelled out instead of one being rescued while the rest stay in the red.

Then each settlement gets the yield you picked for it on its card. Left on [b]Balanced[/b], a city is fed Production and a town Food. Leftovers go by situational fit, then single-yield resources, then multi-yield, and last those that only cheapen units.

What a resource is worth is read from the game's own data, [b]conditions included[/b]: a bonus needing a city, your capital or a certain building is not counted where it would not happen, and neither is the weaker half of an either/or bonus - Fish pays 8 Food with a Port and 4 without, so it only rates well where the Port is.

[hr][/hr]
[h2]Settings[/h2]

Under [b]Options - Mods[/b]: how hard to chase Happiness (never / cities only / all), and what to do when a resource arrives with the screen closed:

[code]Never                        the mod does nothing on its own
Place the new resource       just the one that arrived
Place everything unassigned  the arrival is a cue to tidy the whole pool
Rebuild every assignment     clears every settlement and starts again[/code]

Anything short of the last never moves a resource you already placed, and nothing happens unless something happens in-game.

Two more, on by default: [b]build a Culture settlement[/b] and [b]build a Gold settlement[/b]. Every resource paying that yield is gathered into the one city already making the most of it, where percentages compound. Settlements with a priority of their own come first.

On the screen itself, [b]Imports first[/b] - off by default, for the [b]Economic Victory[/b] only. A resource in a City is worth +1 GDP a turn; an imported one is worth [b]+1 more on top[/b], twice as much as your own, and neither pays in a Town. It outranks every settlement's priority, so chasing any other victory, leave it off.

[hr][/hr]
[h2]Empire resources[/h2]

The Empire tab told you what a resource does - "+1 Gold and Happiness in all settlements" - the same whether you hold one settlement or twenty. Now it says what it is worth:

[code](icon) GOLD [6]
One:  +12 (gold)  +12 (happiness)
All:  +72 (gold)  +72 (happiness)[/code]

Above the tabs, one income total for the empire. Combat bonuses name every unit class they reach, including ones the game's own description omits, and capped or Celebration-only bonuses say so rather than inflating the total.

[hr][/hr]
[h2]Factory resources[/h2]

A Modern-age resource pays nothing for being owned - it only counts once it sits in a settlement with a Factory, and the game has no screen for them. The new tab shows what is [b]in factories[/b] and what it produces, what is [b]idle[/b] and what it would add, and the GDP it earns.

An optional [b]Factories first[/b] switch places them the way the game rewards: a settlement runs one kind at a time but any number of copies, so each factory starts on the kind that can fill it.

[hr][/hr]
[h2]Trade routes and treasure convoys[/h2]

A route card used to bury its destination in a sentence; now the title line says all of it and three fit in a row. One line says how many routes you run and how many you could, with a tooltip naming every leader you have room for - split into those you can sign [b]now[/b] and those out of [b]range[/b].

Treasure convoys get the same layout, homeland settlements leave the "not generating" list, and each shows the [b]Gold[/b] and [b]GDP[/b] it pays.

[hr][/hr]
[h2]Compatibility, source and credits[/h2]

Saved games are unaffected and [b]no rules, values or balance change[/b]. Safe to add or remove mid-game.

⚠️ [b]Not compatible with Resource+.[/b] Both replace the same screen; run one or the other.

Open source, free to reuse: [url=https://github.com/bopielka/better-commerce-screen-ui-civ-7]github.com/bopielka/better-commerce-screen-ui-civ-7[/url]

Buy me a coffee: [url=https://tipply.pl/@najane]tipply.pl/@najane[/url]

